e  the bayley family has owned and operated  the market for more than 130 years  through the 1800s early 1900s  the oyster industry was really what  defined baltimore  a lot of areas would backfill with  oyster shells they used crushed oyster  shells  so we were using the shells  not realizing that they needed to go  back into the bay  it wasn't until the 1950s when  scientists discovered the link to the  bay's declining health  we were depleting our supply and that  these oysters needed old oyster shells  in order to grow new baby oysters this  bat  but with the shells shuck then the  oysters dressed  a little dab of a cocktail and a little  squeeze of lemon there was no easy way  to get the used shells back into the  water  cheers cheers here you go  fantastic they are they are so good so  good they're so good  now we have to recycle these shells  fadeley's is one of about 400  area seafood restaurants markets and  public job sites that has been recycling  their shells through the oyster recovery  partnership  orp crews pick up the shells and bring  them to the university of maryland's  horn point laboratory to be processed  over the next year  when we get the dirty shells they're  aged they're clean and then they're  loaded into the containers and then  placed into the tanks ready for us to  start  putting mature larvae in it stephanie  alexander is the laboratory's hatchery  manager  they filter the water they create  habitat they are also spawning and  adding to the population  and they're also helping with shoreline  stabilization as well as nutrients so  question  so overall they're extremely extremely  valuable they're the little kidneys of  the bay  but these vital organs of the chesapeake  need some assistance to repopulate  at the rate needed to keep the bait  clean so  what we have here is what we have a  bunch of oysters that we don't know the  sex of yet  getting ready to start their  reproduction around  25 thousand adult oysters a year are  rotated through these spawning tables  they're filtering this water in and they  are tasting it  to say hey somebody else is spawning i  need to start spawning a response and  it's 18 reactions  because they have to spawn at the same  time in order for fertilization to take  place  in about two weeks the fertilized eggs  will be mature enough to be drained from  their indoor incubators  gathered for their next stage in life  right now there's five million  larvae in my hand the bundle of larvae  will be set in one of the outdoor tanks  that's  filled with the recycled oyster shells  the goal  to get the larvae to attach to the  shells where they'll grow for life  a process known as spat on shell  after another week or so they're loaded  up onto a boat  and released into the water and then  when they're deployed  they go out there they're put in reefs  is that correct they're put on reefs  which is basically a hard substrate but  we don't want to put them in the mud  because if you put oysters in the mud  they're going to sink and they're going  to die and then all of our efforts for  nothing  orp says they've planted around 8.5  billion juvenile oysters back in the  waters and have rehabbed reefs and two  tributaries with three more to go  set to be completed by 2025. there's a  lot that goes into this funding wise as  well right  absolutely it's a it's definitely a  multi-million dollar  operation to restore oysters annually  in maryland and there's a huge  commitment from the state of maryland to  provide that funding  noaa also provides funds and so does the  u.s army corps of engineers  but manmade challenges are once again  risking the oyster survival  historically over-harvesting and disease  issues were kind of the big driver of  the decline of  oysters now i think it's lack of  suitable substrate  because as the land becomes developed  you have more runoff and in that runoff  you have more sediment and that sediment  is falling out and burying the  oyster reefs shell is very limiting so  we need everything we can get  so there's little steps that everybody  can get involved in you can eat oysters  you can support oyster aquaculture farms  and just make it a big circle of oyster  love  for cbs this morning saturday skyler  henry  cambridge maryland a big circle of  oyster love and the little kidneys in  the bay  i had no idea how important  yeah well it just goes to show you how  sustainability it should be a part of  our practice  as human beings and yet we we thwart it  in so many ways take care of the oysters  they take care of the ocean they take  care of our bellies  yeah that too right i mean it's all it  all works together if you're doing it  the right way  really really great insight and great  program

